n rides the tilted ecliptic, its declination (angular distance from the celestial equator) swings smoothly between +23.4\u00b0 and \u221223.4\u00b0. The extremes are the solstices (farthest north around June 21, farthest south around December 21); the two crossings of the equator are the equinoxes, when the Sun's daily path is bisected by every horizon and day and night are equal everywhere."}, {"id": "q3", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Which fact by itself disproves the idea that the seasons are caused by Earth's changing distance from the Sun?", "options": ["Earth's orbit is a perfect circle, so the distance never changes", "The two hemispheres have OPPOSITE seasons at the same instant, yet the whole Earth is at a single distance from the Sun at any moment \u2014 so distance cannot make them opposite (and, independently, Earth is actually CLOSEST in early January, northern winter, and the distance varies only ~3%, far too little)", "The Sun's output changes with the seasons", "The Earth speeds up in summer and slows down in winter"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Any of three facts is fatal. The hemispheres are opposite at one instant, when the planet is at one distance \u2014 impossible for a distance cause. Earth is nearest the Sun (perihelion, ~0.983 AU) in early January, the depth of northern winter \u2014 the timing is backwards. And the ~3% distance change gives only ~7% variation in sunlight, far too small for the several-fold seasonal swing. The distance theory is plausible but collapses the instant you check it against the geometry and the numbers."}, {"id": "q4", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "By what TWO mechanisms does the axial tilt actually cause the seasons?", "options": ["It changes the Sun's brightness and the Earth's orbital speed", "As a FIXED tilt is carried around the orbit, each hemisphere leans sunward then away \u2014 changing DIRECTNESS (the noon Sun rides high in summer, concentrating light on a small patch; low in winter, smearing it thin \u2014 intensity \u221d sine of altitude) and DURATION (long summer days, short winter days) \u2014 and the two reinforce each other", "It moves the Earth closer to the Sun in summer and farther in winter", "It tilts the Sun's rays so they reflect off the atmosphere in winter"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "Earth's axis holds a fixed direction in space (toward Polaris) as it orbits, so half the year a hemisphere leans toward the Sun and half away. Leaning sunward raises the noon Sun, so its rays strike more straight-down and concentrate their energy (directness, scaling as the sine of altitude), AND keeps it above the horizon longer (duration). Both effects come from the same tilt and multiply: summer is high-and-long sun, winter is low-and-brief. No change in distance is involved."}, {"id": "q5", "type": "mcq", "prompt": "Where do the tropics (23.4\u00b0) and polar circles (66.5\u00b0) come from?", "options": ["They mark where the oceans are warmest and coldest", "The Sun can stand directly overhead only where latitude equals its declination, and the declination only reaches \u00b123.4\u00b0, so the TROPICS sit at \u00b123.4\u00b0; the POLAR CIRCLES at \u00b166.5\u00b0 = \u00b1(90\u00b0 \u2212 23.4\u00b0) mark where the solstice Sun just stays up all day (midnight Sun) or down all day (polar night) \u2014 every one of these lines is set by the obliquity", "They are the boundaries of the Earth's magnetic field", "They were chosen arbitrarily by mapmakers"], "answerIndex": 1, "explanation": "The Sun reaches the zenith only when your latitude matches its declination, and since the declination tops out at \u00b123.4\u00b0, only latitudes within \u00b123.4\u00b0 \u2014 the tropics \u2014 ever get an overhead Sun. At 90\u00b0 \u2212 23.4\u00b0 = 66.5\u00b0, the solstice Sun's daily circle just grazes the horizon for a full 24 hours: the polar circles, edge of the midnight Sun and polar night. Change the tilt and every one of these lines \u2014 and the climate zones they bound \u2014 moves; the geography of climate is the obliquity written onto the globe."}, {"id": "q6", "type": "open", "prompt": "The 'distance causes seasons' idea is the most widespread misconception in everyday astronomy. State it, give the three independent facts that each disprove it, and then explain the real cause \u2014 the two mechanisms of the tilt \u2014 and why demolishing the wrong answer teaches the course's central skill.", "rubric": "THE MISCONCEPTION: the seasons are caused by Earth's changing DISTANCE from the Sun \u2014 closer in summer (hot), farther in winter (cold). It is seductive because it borrows a true everyday intuition (closer to a heat source is warmer) and is held even by many well-educated people. THREE INDEPENDENTLY FATAL FACTS: (1) WRONG DIRECTION \u2014 Earth is actually CLOSEST to the Sun (perihelion, ~0.983 AU) in EARLY JANUARY, the depth of northern winter, and farthest (aphelion, ~1.017 AU) in early July, northern summer; if distance drove the seasons, northern winter would be summer. (2) WRONG MAGNITUDE \u2014 the distance varies only ~3% over the year, which by the inverse-square law changes received sunlight only ~7%, nowhere near the several-fold summer/winter difference in energy per unit area. (3) OPPOSITE HEMISPHERES \u2014 the Northern and Southern Hemispheres have OPPOSITE seasons AT THE SAME INSTANT, yet the whole Earth is at a SINGLE distance from the Sun at any moment, so distance cannot make them opposite (if it did, both hemispheres would have summer together). Any one fact kills the theory; together they are overwhelming. THE REAL CAUSE \u2014 THE TILT, VIA TWO MECHANISMS: Earth's axis is tipped 23.4\u00b0 and holds a FIXED direction in space (toward Polaris) as Earth orbits, so each hemisphere leans TOWARD the Sun for half the year and AWAY for the other half. (a) DIRECTNESS: leaning sunward raises the noon Sun; its rays strike nearly straight down and concentrate energy on a small patch of ground (intense heating); leaning away, the low Sun's rays strike at a glancing angle and smear the same beam over a large area (weak heating) \u2014 intensity scales as the SINE of the Sun's altitude (flashlight straight down = small bright circle; grazing = large dim ellipse). (b) DURATION: leaning sunward keeps the Sun above the horizon LONGER (long summer days, more hours of heating); leaning away gives short winter days. The two REINFORCE: summer = higher AND longer sun (double dose), winter = lower AND briefer (double reduction), multiplying into the large seasonal swing \u2014 with NO distance change. THE SKILL: demolishing the wrong answer teaches the course's core method \u2014 a PLAUSIBLE explanation consistent with intuition can be completely WRONG, and the only way to find out is to check it against the actual GEOMETRY and NUMBERS (ask 'when is Earth actually closest?' and it collapses); reading the true geometry from appearances, and refusing an explanation just because it sounds reasonable, is exactly the discipline the whole course is training. A strong answer keeps the small, real distance effect (a ~7% modulation) distinct from the dominant cause (the tilt)."}, {"id": "q7", "type": "open", "prompt": "Show how the gnomon \u2014 a vertical stick and its shadow \u2014 let the ancients read the seasons and even measure the 23.4\u00b0 tilt, and explain why this is 'the course's through-line made literal.' Then explain how that same tilt carves the tropics and polar circles onto the globe.", "rubric": "THE GNOMON: a vertical stick (the oldest astronomical instrument) casts a shadow whose length reads the Sun's ALTITUDE \u2014 a high Sun casts a SHORT shadow, a low Sun a LONG one (L = h/tan(altitude)). The SHORTEST shadow of each day falls at LOCAL NOON, and it does two things: it points TRUE NORTH (the noon Sun is due south in the northern hemisphere, so the shadow points north \u2014 finding the cardinal directions with no compass), and its length measures the noon Sun's altitude that day. READING THE SEASONS: track that noon shadow across a YEAR and the seasons appear in its length \u2014 SHORTEST at the SUMMER solstice (highest Sun), LONGEST at the WINTER solstice (lowest Sun), in between at the equinoxes. This is literally what 'solstice' (sol + sistere, 'sun stands still') means: the day the noon shadow stops shrinking and starts to grow again. Ancient cultures (Babylonian, Egyptian, Greek, Chinese) fixed the solstices/equinoxes and built calendars this way; the great stone alignments (Stonehenge and many others) are elaborate gnomons. MEASURING THE TILT: measure the noon Sun's altitude at BOTH solstices and take HALF THE DIFFERENCE, and you get the OBLIQUITY \u2014 e.g. at 40\u00b0N, (73.4\u00b0 \u2212 26.6\u00b0)/2 = 23.4\u00b0 \u2014 the tilt of the Earth's axis, extracted from two shadow-lengths six months apart (the Greeks, e.g. Eratosthenes, did essentially this, getting ~23.85\u00b0), centuries before anyone knew the Earth 'had' an axis. WHY THROUGH-LINE MADE LITERAL: the whole course's thesis is 'every apparent motion/appearance is the shadow of a real geometry, and astronomy is the art of reading the geometry from the shadow' \u2014 and here it is with actual light and shadow: the lean of the world, read off the ground from a stick's shadow. Every later instrument (telescope, spectroscope, space probe) is, in a sense, a more elaborate gnomon for reading the cosmos's real geometry from the appearances it casts. THE TILT CARVES THE GLOBE: because the Sun's declination reaches only \u00b123.4\u00b0, and the Sun stands OVERHEAD only where latitude = declination, the Sun can be at the zenith only between \u00b123.4\u00b0 \u2014 the TROPICS (Cancer 23.4\u00b0N, overhead at June solstice; Capricorn 23.4\u00b0S, overhead at December solstice); poleward of them it never is. At \u00b1(90\u00b0 \u2212 23.4\u00b0) = \u00b166.5\u00b0 sit the POLAR CIRCLES (Arctic/Antarctic), where the solstice Sun just stays up a full 24 hours (the MIDNIGHT SUN) or stays down all day (POLAR NIGHT). These four lines, and the tropical/temperate/polar climate zones they bound, are set entirely by the obliquity \u2014 change the tilt and you redraw the map (a bigger tilt widens the tropics/polar zones and sharpens seasons; smaller mutes them). A strong answer connects both halves: a stick's shadow both reveals the seasons and measures the very tilt that draws the planet's climate geography, a vivid instance of reading real geometry from a shadow."}]$astro2_quiz$::jsonb as questions
)
insert into quizzes (node_id, level, questions, generated)
select 'astronomy-and-space/foundations/observed-sky/seasons', lvl, q.questions, true
from q, (values ('easy'), ('advanced'), ('expert')) as l(lvl)
on conflict (node_id, level) do update
  set questions = excluded.questions, generated = excluded.generated;
